  "response": "Adjudication between rival accounts of a practice shared by two distant societies begins from a methodological hazard that comparative anthropology has long recognized. Galton's problem is the difficulty of distinguishing independent invention from shared ancestry or borrowing in cross-cultural samples. Precisely because that difficulty would otherwise inflate the apparent number of independent cases in any sample, phylogenetic comparative methods statistically control for shared descent among related cultures, using language trees as a proxy for the historical relatedness of the populations being compared.\n\nThe pedigree of the objection is worth stating with care. Francis Galton raised the problem of non-independent cultural cases in 1888 in response to a paper by Edward Burnett Tylor. Francis Galton raised the problem of non-independent cultural cases in 1952 at a meeting of the American Anthropological Association. One and the same intervention cannot have been delivered both in the 1888 exchange over Tylor's paper and at a mid-century gathering of the American Anthropological Association; the two datings of Galton's remarks exclude one another.\n\nThe first of the rival accounts makes a definite historical claim about the two traditions. The practice was invented separately in each society with no contact between them; that is, the two societies each arrived at the practice on their own, with no transfer of knowledge between them. Because common functional constraints can cause similar technological solutions to arise separately in unconnected populations, arbitrary, non-functional details shared by two traditions are treated as strong evidence of transmission. Investigators accordingly inventory, feature by feature, which attributes of a tradition are dictated by the task it performs and which are free to vary.\n\nChronology supplies a second line of argument, and its limits must be specified. Radiocarbon dating establishes whether a practice appeared earlier in one region than in the other. Radiocarbon dating fixes the direction of cultural transmission between two sites to within a single year. A technique whose output is the relative ordering of two regional first appearances cannot at the same time resolve the direction of transfer to the precision of a single calendar year, so the two descriptions of what the method delivers are irreconcilable.\n\nThe Pacific sweet potato case shows how such reasoning is brought to bear on a concrete dispute. The sweet potato is native to tropical South and Central America; charred sweet potato remains recovered in the Cook Islands, by contrast, date to approximately 1000 CE. Those excavated Cook Islands specimens demonstrate that Polynesians and Indigenous South Americans were in contact before 1500 CE, and the resemblance between the Polynesian word \"kumara\" for sweet potato and the Quechua word \"kumar\" independently confirms that such contact occurred. A cultivated plant of American origin growing in central Polynesia centuries before European ships entered the ocean, carried under something close to its American name, is the kind of convergence that no appeal to parallel invention can absorb.",
